WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.379
In this video, we're going to cover what

2
00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:04.860
Open Sound Control is,

3
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.599
where it came from, and its many use cases.

4
00:00:07.599 --> 00:00:10.250
Open Sound Control is

5
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.810
a data transport specification similar to MIDI or DMX,

6
00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.269
and it's for real-time message communication

7
00:00:17.269 --> 00:00:20.410
among many different kinds of application and hardware.

8
00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.030
This is often referred to as peer-to-peer messaging,

9
00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.250
and this works not just in Unreal Engine,

10
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.489
but in a wide variety of different softwares,

11
00:00:27.489 --> 00:00:32.250
including QLab, TouchOSC, MaxMSP, and many, many more.

12
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.609
We've used OSC in many cases,

13
00:00:34.609 --> 00:00:37.229
not just to send simple messages back and forth,

14
00:00:37.229 --> 00:00:40.530
but even to utilize a fairly robust multiplayer setup

15
00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.890
without having to deal with the limitations

16
00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.109
of a server-client relationship in a traditional sense,

17
00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:48.689
such as where when a server crashes,

18
00:00:48.689 --> 00:00:51.609
the clients are no longer able to stay in a session.

19
00:00:51.609 --> 00:00:53.649
Through OSC, as you'll see,

20
00:00:53.649 --> 00:00:55.770
we can send signals as needed,

21
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.890
and if anything goes wrong with those signals,

22
00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.649
they can simply be sent again,

23
00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:02.649
and because none of the quote-unquote clients

24
00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:05.290
inside the session are actually being hosted

25
00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.609
by a traditional server, there's no problem

26
00:01:07.609 --> 00:01:09.689
if anything goes wrong with that server.

27
00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:13.170
It can always come back and send those messages once more.

28
00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.989
To add on to that,

29
00:01:14.989 --> 00:01:18.930
the way we use it to replace our traditional multiplayer,

30
00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.890
OSC give us a lot more flexibility.

31
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.730
We can be selective about the things we want to send,

32
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.370
and because we're not bind to a traditional server,

33
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:33.349
we can be very flexible about our session.

34
00:01:33.349 --> 00:01:35.650
Sometimes we can have a session still running

35
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:36.750
without a server.

36
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.329
We can have multiple computer being the server.

37
00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:44.049
That's a lot of more flexibility and benefit

38
00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:49.310
comes from using OSC as a communication method.

39
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.849
OSC, again, refers to open sound control,

40
00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:54.090
which is unfortunately a bit of a misnomer

41
00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.769
because OSC can be used for a wide variety of purposes.

42
00:01:57.769 --> 00:02:00.450
We can send integers, strings, floats,

43
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.549
a wide variety of different kinds of signals

44
00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:04.370
besides just sound,

45
00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.650
but OSC was basically developed

46
00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:08.930
to be an alternative to MIDI.

47
00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.569
Anyone who's played on a keyboard piano before

48
00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:12.569
has probably heard of MIDI

49
00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:15.729
in the way that you can send music signals to a computer.

50
00:02:15.729 --> 00:02:17.449
Well, MIDI was pretty limited

51
00:02:17.449 --> 00:02:20.030
in terms of how it was able to send messages,

52
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:23.949
so OSC was designed essentially to be a more robust way

53
00:02:23.949 --> 00:02:27.289
to send different kinds of messages with more complexity

54
00:02:27.289 --> 00:02:29.789
and is now widely used across a wide variety

55
00:02:29.789 --> 00:02:30.990
of industries.

56
00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.830
Some of the benefits of OSC compared to MIDI

57
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.569
include a higher resolution

58
00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:38.430
because OSC can send full floating point numbers

59
00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.870
and a wide variety of other data types,

60
00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.169
and MIDI 7-bit messaging is highly limited

61
00:02:43.169 --> 00:02:44.669
by what it can do.

62
00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:45.949
We have flexible addressing

63
00:02:45.949 --> 00:02:48.590
because OSC messages can be structured with a hierarchy,

64
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.930
kind of like URLs,

65
00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.590
which allows for much more organized

66
00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.289
and descriptive communication.

67
00:02:53.289 --> 00:02:54.590
We also have extensibility

68
00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:57.050
because users can define their own message formats

69
00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:58.830
and address spaces,

70
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.830
as you'll see the way that a OSC message is sent

71
00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.270
looks a lot like a directory or URL,

72
00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:05.509
and that makes it adaptable

73
00:03:05.509 --> 00:03:07.270
to a wide variety of applications

74
00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.189
beyond just sound and music.

75
00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:10.590
And lastly, here we mentioned

76
00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.710
that we have network centric design

77
00:03:12.710 --> 00:03:14.289
because OSC was designed

78
00:03:14.289 --> 00:03:16.750
with a modern networking infrastructure,

79
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.349
well past when MIDI was created

80
00:03:18.949 --> 00:03:19.909
where networking was much more limited.

81
00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:21.710
And so it's a well-suited format

82
00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.750
for any kind of internet or land-based communication.

83
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.590
And what June and I will be showing you today

84
00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:28.770
involves both sending messages locally

85
00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.110
when you're on the same subnet,

86
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.110
as well as opening up ports

87
00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:36.659
and sending data across the internet.

88
00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:37.939
As previously mentioned,

89
00:03:37.939 --> 00:03:41.159
OSC can be used across a very wide variety of industries

90
00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:42.620
such as music production,

91
00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.139
theatrical and live show control, performance capture,

92
00:03:45.139 --> 00:03:47.580
virtual production, and interactive art.

93
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:48.500
Here on this page,

94
00:03:48.539 --> 00:03:50.120
we have an example of a show

95
00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.800
that June and I were involved with

96
00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.099
with our company Agile Lens a few years ago,

97
00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:55.860
a Christmas Carol VR.

98
00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.020
And the way that this show was able to function

99
00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.419
was cues were able to fire

100
00:04:00.419 --> 00:04:03.419
from another piece of software called TouchOSC,

101
00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:05.500
basically mimicking the same cue board

102
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:09.259
that we had constructed inside Unreal Engine 4.27,

103
00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:11.259
which meant that if there was anything wrong

104
00:04:11.259 --> 00:04:14.379
with the host who was sending out cues in Unreal Engine,

105
00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:17.100
we could actually send out exactly the same signals

106
00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.480
from this other software,

107
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.620
which could run on Windows or Mac

108
00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.879
or even something like an iPad,

109
00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:25.180
just allowing for a much more robust

110
00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:28.819
and flexible way to control the show.

111
00:04:28.819 --> 00:04:29.660
In the next video,

112
00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:32.100
we'll look at some of the core concepts of OSC,

113
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.420
including how it gets implemented directly

114
00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:35.560
in Unreal Engine.
